1. Min Cost Climbing Stairs

link - https://leetcode.com/problems/min-cost-climbing-stairs/description/

solution
 bottom-up approach

 class Solution {
    public int minCostClimbingStairs(int[] cost) {
        int n=cost.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        dp[0]=cost[0]; // min cost to reach stair 0
        dp[1]=cost[1]; // cost to reach stair 1

        for(int i=2;i<n;i++){
            dp[i]=cost[i]+Math.min(dp[i-1],dp[i-2]); // minimum cost to stand stair i 
            //cost[i] added because in quetion it says - where cost[i] is the cost of ith step on a staircase
            // and we can come from i-1 or i-2 position
        } 

        return Math.min(dp[n-1],dp[n-2]); // dp[n] -> above last stair
    }
}

TC - O(N)   SC - O(N)


2. minimum path sum
link - https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-path-sum/description/

class Solution {
    public int minPathSum(int[][] grid) {

        int m = grid.length;
        int n = grid[0].length;

        // first row
        for(int j = 1; j < n; j++) {
            grid[0][j] += grid[0][j - 1];
        }

        // first column
        for(int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            grid[i][0] += grid[i - 1][0];
        }

        // remaining cells
        for(int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            for(int j = 1; j < n; j++) {

                grid[i][j] += Math.min(grid[i - 1][j],
                                       grid[i][j - 1]);
            }
        }

        return grid[m - 1][n - 1];
    }
}

TC - O(m*n)   SC - O(1) (in-place modification of grid)

3. House Robber
link - https://leetcode.com/problems/house-robber/description/

class Solution {
    public int rob(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n == 0) return 0;
        if (n == 1) return nums[0];

        int[] dp = new int[n];
        dp[0] = nums[0];
        dp[1] = Math.max(nums[0], nums[1]);

        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i - 1], nums[i] + dp[i - 2]);
        }

        return dp[n - 1];
    }
}

TC - O(N)   SC - O(N)

Explanation:
- We create a dp array where dp[i] represents the maximum amount of money that can be robbed from the first i houses.
- The base cases are:  
  - dp[0] = nums[0] (if there's only one house, rob it)  
  - dp[1] = max(nums[0], nums[1]) (for two houses, rob the one with more money)     

- For each subsequent house i, we have two choices:
  - Do not rob house i: In this case, the maximum amount remains dp[i - 1].
  - Rob house i: In this case, we add the money from house i (nums[i]) to the maximum amount from two houses back (dp[i - 2]) since we cannot rob adjacent houses.
- We take the maximum of these two choices to fill dp[i].   
- Finally, dp[n - 1] will contain the maximum amount of money that can be robbed from all the houses.




4. House Robber II

link - https://leetcode.com/problems/house-robber-ii/description/   

class Solution {
    public int rob(int[] nums) {

        int n = nums.length;
        if(n==1) return nums[0]; // if there is only one house, rob it
        
        int ans1 = houseRobber(nums,0,n-2); // if we rob the first house, we cannot rob the last house, so we consider the range from 0 to n-2
        int ans2 = houseRobber(nums,1,n-1); // if we do not rob the first house, we can consider the range from 1 to n-1, which includes the last house

        return Math.max(ans1,ans2);
        
    }

    public int houseRobber(int nums[], int start, int end){
        int n = end - start + 1;
        int[] dp = new int[n];

        if(n==1) return nums[start]; // if there is only one house in the range, rob it

        dp[0]=nums[start];
        dp[1]=Math.max(nums[start],nums[start+1]);

        for(int i=2;i<n;i++){
            dp[i]=Math.max(nums[start+i]+dp[i-2],dp[i-1]); // for each house, we have two choices: rob it and add its value to the maximum amount from two houses back (dp[i-2]), or skip it and take the maximum amount from the previous house (dp[i-1]). We take the maximum of these two choices to fill dp[i].
        }

        return dp[n-1];
    }

}

TC- O(N) and SC - O(N)

Explanation:
- Since the houses are arranged in a circle, we cannot rob both the first and last house. Therefore, we have two scenarios to consider:
  1. Rob the first house and skip the last house: In this case, we can only consider the houses from index 0 to n-2.
  2. Skip the first house and rob the last house: In this case, we can consider the houses from index 1 to n-1. 
- We define a helper function `houseRobber` that takes a range of houses and applies the same logic as the original House Robber problem to calculate the maximum amount that can be robbed from that range.
- Finally, we return the maximum of the two scenarios to get the overall maximum amount that can be robbed from the circular arrangement of houses.


5. Longest pallindrome subsequence
link - https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-palindromic-subsequence/description/

Brute force approach - generate all subsequences and check if they are palindrome, return the length of longest pallindrome subsequence 

public class Solution {
    public String longestPalindrome(String s) {
        if (s.length() <= 1) {
            return s;
        }

        int maxLen = 1;
        String maxStr = s.substring(0, 1);

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            for (int j = i + maxLen+1; j <= s.length(); j++) {
                if (j - i > maxLen && isPalindrome(s.substring(i, j))) {
                    maxLen = j - i;
                    maxStr = s.substring(i, j);
                }
            }
        }

        return maxStr;
    }

    private boolean isPalindrome(String str) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = str.length() - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            if (str.charAt(left) != str.charAt(right)) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return true;
    }
}

explanation:
- We iterate through all possible substrings of the input string `s` using two nested loops. The outer loop starts from the first character and the inner loop starts from the next character after the current position of the outer loop.
- For each substring, we check if its length is greater than the current maximum length (`maxLen`) and if it is a palindrome by calling the `isPalindrome` helper function.
- If the substring is a palindrome and its length is greater than `maxLen`, we update `maxLen` and store the substring in `maxStr`.
- Finally, we return `maxStr`, which contains the longest palindromic substring found.  
TC - O(N^3)   SC - O(N) (for the substring and palindrome check)

using dynamic programming approach
